# Owain Gwynedd: the longer text for his person page (person-owain-gwynedd.html), with the part
# "The king who became prince": how and why King Owain took the title princeps, its Roman roots,
# the Welsh idea of a tywysog, why Henry II was angry, and the princes who followed.
# long.py pulls this in. House rule (EDITORIAL.md, "Kings are kings"): Owain is a king. He is called
# prince only where the text is about the title he chose for himself.
# Sources checked (November 2026): Huw Pryce, "Owain Gwynedd and Louis VII: the Franco-Welsh diplomacy of
# the first Prince of Wales", Welsh History Review 19 (1998), and The Acts of Welsh Rulers 1120-1283 (2005);
# Becket's letter to Pope Alexander III (Correspondence of Thomas Becket, ed. Duggan, I, no. 12);
# Dictionary of Welsh Biography; J. B. Smith, Llywelyn ap Gruffudd, Prince of Wales.
# Paragraph codes (see prose() in build.py): "## Heading", "> Quote | Source", "|| Left | Right".

PERSON_LONG = (
 ["King Owain ap Gruffudd ruled Gwynedd for thirty-three years, from 1137 to 1170, and Wales remembers him by the name of his kingdom: Owain Gwynedd. He beat the Normans at Crug Mawr, carried his border east to the Dee, and in 1165 led the rulers of Wales together against a huge army gathered by Henry II, and sent it home. The chronicle of the Welsh, the Annales Cambriae, calls him rex, king. He was also the first ruler of Wales to take a new and greater title: Prince of the Welsh.",
  "|| Born | about 1100",
  "|| Father | Gruffudd ap Cynan, king of Gwynedd",
  "|| Mother | Angharad, daughter of Owain ab Edwin",
  "|| King of Gwynedd | 1137–1170",
  "|| Great victories | Crug Mawr, 1136; the Berwyn, 1165",
  "|| His titles | king of Wales (rex Wallie); then Prince of Wales (Waliarum princeps) and Prince of the Welsh (Wallensium princeps)",
  "|| Died | November 1170",
  "|| Buried | Bangor Cathedral",
  "## The son of Gruffudd ap Cynan",
  "Owain was a son of Gruffudd ap Cynan, the king who had won Gwynedd at Mynydd Carn in 1081, and of Angharad ferch Owain. As his father grew old, Owain and his brother Cadwaladr led the warband of Gwynedd. In 1136, when the death of Henry I of England set Wales alight, the brothers marched south into Ceredigion. At Crug Mawr near Cardigan they met the Norman lords of the south and broke them, and the fleeing Normans crowded onto the bridge over the Teifi until it gave way beneath them.",
  "When Gruffudd died in 1137, old and blind, Owain became king.",
  "## King of Gwynedd",
  "Owain made good use of the years when England was torn apart by the war between Stephen and Matilda. He took Mold in 1146 and Iâl in 1149, where he raised the castle mound of Tomen y Rhodwydd, and his kingdom ran east to the estuary of the Dee.",
  "In 1157 the new king of England, Henry II, came to win it back. In the woods of Coed Eulo, Owain’s sons fell on the royal army, and Henry barely escaped with his life. Owain was wise enough to make peace and wait for his moment.",
  "## 1165: all Wales together",
  "In 1165 Henry gathered an army from England and from his lands across the sea, with ships hired from Dublin, to crush the Welsh for good. For once every Welsh ruler stood together, and they stood with Owain. The men of Gwynedd, the Lord Rhys with the men of Deheubarth, and the princes of Powys gathered at Corwen. Henry climbed onto the Berwyn from Oswestry, and there the weather of Wales fought for Wales: the rain fell for days, the tracks turned to mud, Welsh raiders cut off his supplies, and the king turned back without a battle. He never invaded Gwynedd again.",
  "Owain followed up his victory. He took Basingwerk in 1166 and the castles of Rhuddlan and Prestatyn in 1167, and his border stood on the Dee once more.",
  "## The king who became prince",
  "It was in these years, the proudest of his reign, that King Owain changed the way he named himself. Early on he wrote as a king. In a letter to Bernard, bishop of St Davids, he styled himself rex Wallie, king of Wales, and in his first letters to the court of King Louis VII of France he wrote again as king of Wales and king of the Welsh.",
  "Then, in the middle of the 1160s, he took a new title. In his second letter to Louis he named himself Waliarum princeps, Prince of Wales. It is the oldest use of that title by a Welsh ruler that survives anywhere. In the letters of Archbishop Thomas Becket he is the prince of the Welsh, princeps Wallensium. The poets and the chronicle in Welsh called him tywysog. The title Prince of Wales begins with Owain Gwynedd.",
  "Three of his letters to France survive, two to Louis himself and one to his chancellor. No Welsh ruler before him is known to have reached out to a king of France. In them Owain tells Louis of the war of 1165, offers him friendship and help against Henry, and asks for his support in return. In 1168 Owain’s envoys, led by Arthur of Bardsey, bishop of Bangor, carried his messages to France.",
  "## Why he did it",
  "It can look strange today, a king taking a title that sounds smaller. In Owain’s world it was the opposite. Historians give several reasons, and they fit together.",
  "The first is that king had become too small a word in Wales. There had been many kings in Wales: kings of Gwynedd, of Powys, of Deheubarth, of Morgannwg, each ruling his own land, and to be a king was to be one of many. Princeps meant something none of them could claim alone. It meant the first, the one who stood above all the others, as Augustus had stood over Rome. Owain was saying that he led all the Welsh, not Gwynedd alone. After 1165, when the rulers of Wales had gathered with him at Corwen, it was a claim the facts upheld.",
  "> The title was held by only one ruler, literally the chief ruler of Wales. | Huw Pryce, historian of the Welsh princes",
  "The second is that it was a step up, not a step down. In the Latin of twelfth-century Europe a princeps was a ruler in his own right, with no lord above him in his own land. In the France of Louis VII the great rulers who governed their own countries, the dukes of Normandy and Aquitaine and the counts of Flanders and Champagne, were the princes of the realm. By taking the title, Owain set himself among the great rulers of Europe, and told a foreign king that Wales had its own head who answered to no one.",
  "The third is that king of Wales would have meant open war over titles. Henry II claimed to be the overlord of every ruler in Wales, and in 1163 he had made them do homage to him at Woodstock. A crowned king of all Wales would have been a rival crown in Britain, a challenge that Henry, the Church and the court of France would all have resisted. Prince made the same claim to lead the Welsh, in a form the rulers of Europe could accept. Even so, it was more than Henry could bear.",
  "The fourth is that it fitted the Welsh idea of a tywysog. The Welsh word comes from tywys, to lead: a tywysog is one who goes before the people and leads them. Owain called himself prince of the Welsh, Wallensium, the leader of a people, and not only the lord of a stretch of land. The chronicle of the age is named for such leaders: Brut y Tywysogion, the Chronicle of the Princes.",
  "## A title from Rome",
  "The word itself came from Rome. When Augustus made himself master of the Roman world after 31 BC, he would not call himself king. The Romans had driven out their last king, Tarquin the Proud, almost five hundred years before, and the very word rex was hated in Rome. So Augustus took the title princeps, the first citizen, the first man of the state. It comes from primus, first, and capere, to take: the one who takes the first place. The rule of Augustus and of the emperors after him is called the Principate, and princeps became a word for the supreme ruler.",
  "Owain’s clerks wrote in Latin, the language of Rome and of the Church, and they knew what the word carried. Wales had never forgotten Rome: Welsh kings traced their lines to Macsen Wledig, the emperor Magnus Maximus, and the Welsh remembered themselves as the Britons, citizens of Rome before the English came. A Welsh ruler who called himself princeps was claiming the oldest title of authority in the Latin world: the first among all, answering to no one above him.",
  "## Why King Henry was angry",
  "Henry II did not like it at all. He held that every ruler in Wales was his vassal. A king of Gwynedd could be one vassal among many. A prince of the Welsh was something else: the head of a nation, speaking for all of Wales. And Owain was writing to Louis VII, Henry’s great rival, the king to whom Henry himself owed homage for Normandy. A Prince of Wales in alliance with France could threaten Henry from the west while Louis pressed him in France.",
  "The quarrel reached the Pope. Thomas Becket, Archbishop of Canterbury, wrote to Pope Alexander III that the ‘self-styled Prince Owain’ had left the king ‘greatly angered and annoyed’.",
  "Owain stood his ground against the Church of England too. When the see of Bangor fell empty, he would not take a bishop chosen in England. His own choice, Arthur of Bardsey, was consecrated in Ireland, and the church of Gwynedd stayed in Welsh hands. Neither king nor archbishop could make him give way, and when he died in November 1170 he was buried in Bangor Cathedral.",
  "## The princes who came after",
  "The title Owain chose became the title of Wales. His grandson Llywelyn ab Iorwerth, Llywelyn Fawr, ruled as prince of North Wales, and at Aberdyfi in 1216 the other rulers of Wales did homage to him. From 1230 he styled himself Prince of Aberffraw and Lord of Snowdon, naming the royal seat of Gwynedd on Ynys Môn and the mountains at its heart. His son Dafydd made Prince of Wales his formal title in 1244.",
  "Then came Llywelyn ap Gruffudd, Llywelyn ein Llyw Olaf. He took the title Prince of Wales in 1258, and on 29 September 1267, at the Treaty of Montgomery, King Henry III of England recognised him and his heirs as Prince of Wales, with the homage of the other Welsh lords. Henry III was the grandson of Henry II. A hundred years after the grandfather had raged at the title, the grandson put his seal to it.",
  "In 1400 Owain Glyndŵr was proclaimed Prince of Wales at Glyndyfrdwy, and the title rode out for Wales once more. It had begun with another Owain: the King of Gwynedd who chose to be called the first of the Welsh.",
  "## His family and his poets",
  "Gwalchmai ap Meilyr and Cynddelw Brydydd Mawr sang his praise, and his son Hywel ab Owain Gwynedd was one of the finest poets of the age. Welsh tradition holds that another son, Madog, sailed west from Llandrillo and found a land across the ocean. Through his grandson Llywelyn Fawr, the blood of Owain Gwynedd ran in every Prince of Wales of the house of Aberffraw."],
 ["Rheolodd y Brenin Owain ap Gruffudd Wynedd am dair blynedd ar ddeg ar hugain, o 1137 hyd 1170, ac mae Cymru’n ei gofio wrth enw ei deyrnas: Owain Gwynedd. Trechodd y Normaniaid yng Nghrug Mawr, gwthiodd ei ffin i’r dwyrain at afon Dyfrdwy, ac yn 1165 arweiniodd reolwyr Cymru gyda’i gilydd yn erbyn byddin enfawr a gasglwyd gan Harri’r Ail, a’i gyrru adref. Mae cronicl y Cymry, yr Annales Cambriae, yn ei alw’n rex, brenin. Ef hefyd oedd y rheolwr cyntaf yng Nghymru i gymryd teitl newydd a mwy: Tywysog y Cymry.",
  "|| Ganwyd | tua 1100",
  "|| Tad | Gruffudd ap Cynan, brenin Gwynedd",
  "|| Mam | Angharad, merch Owain ab Edwin",
  "|| Brenin Gwynedd | 1137–1170",
  "|| Buddugoliaethau mawr | Crug Mawr, 1136; y Berwyn, 1165",
  "|| Ei deitlau | brenin Cymru (rex Wallie); yna Tywysog Cymru (Waliarum princeps) a Thywysog y Cymry (Wallensium princeps)",
  "|| Bu farw | Tachwedd 1170",
  "|| Claddwyd | Eglwys Gadeiriol Bangor",
  "## Mab Gruffudd ap Cynan",
  "Roedd Owain yn fab i Gruffudd ap Cynan, y brenin a enillodd Wynedd ym Mynydd Carn yn 1081, ac i Angharad ferch Owain. Wrth i’w dad heneiddio, Owain a’i frawd Cadwaladr a arweiniai osgordd Gwynedd. Yn 1136, pan daniodd marwolaeth Harri I, brenin Lloegr, Gymru gyfan, gorymdeithiodd y brodyr i’r de i Geredigion. Yng Nghrug Mawr ger Aberteifi cyfarfuant ag arglwyddi Normanaidd y de a’u chwalu, a heidiodd y Normaniaid a ffoai ar y bont dros afon Teifi nes iddi dorri danynt.",
  "Pan fu farw Gruffudd yn 1137, yn hen ac yn ddall, daeth Owain yn frenin.",
  "## Brenin Gwynedd",
  "Gwnaeth Owain ddefnydd da o’r blynyddoedd pan rwygwyd Lloegr gan y rhyfel rhwng Steffan a Matilda. Cipiodd yr Wyddgrug yn 1146 ac Iâl yn 1149, lle cododd domen Tomen y Rhodwydd, ac ymestynnai ei deyrnas i’r dwyrain at aber afon Dyfrdwy.",
  "Yn 1157 daeth brenin newydd Lloegr, Harri’r Ail, i’w hennill yn ôl. Yng nghoed Coed Eulo ymosododd meibion Owain ar fyddin y brenin, a phrin y dihangodd Harri â’i fywyd. Roedd Owain yn ddigon doeth i wneud heddwch ac aros am ei gyfle.",
  "## 1165: Cymru gyfan gyda’i gilydd",
  "Yn 1165 casglodd Harri fyddin o Loegr ac o’i diroedd dros y môr, gyda llongau wedi’u llogi o Ddulyn, i fathru’r Cymry unwaith ac am byth. Am unwaith safodd pob un o reolwyr Cymru gyda’i gilydd, a safasant gydag Owain. Ymgasglodd gwŷr Gwynedd, yr Arglwydd Rhys gyda gwŷr Deheubarth, a thywysogion Powys yng Nghorwen. Dringodd Harri i’r Berwyn o Groesoswallt, ac yno ymladdodd tywydd Cymru dros Gymru: glawiodd am ddyddiau, troes y llwybrau’n llaid, torrodd ymosodwyr Cymreig ei gyflenwadau, a throes y brenin yn ôl heb frwydr. Ni ymosododd ar Wynedd byth eto.",
  "Dilynodd Owain ei fuddugoliaeth. Cipiodd Ddinas Basing yn 1166 a chestyll Rhuddlan a Phrestatyn yn 1167, a safai ei ffin ar afon Dyfrdwy unwaith eto.",
  "## Y brenin a ddaeth yn dywysog",
  "Yn y blynyddoedd hyn, balchaf ei deyrnasiad, y newidiodd y Brenin Owain y ffordd yr enwai ei hun. Ar y dechrau ysgrifennai fel brenin. Mewn llythyr at Bernard, esgob Tyddewi, galwodd ei hun yn rex Wallie, brenin Cymru, ac yn ei lythyrau cyntaf at lys y Brenin Louis VII o Ffrainc ysgrifennodd eto fel brenin Cymru a brenin y Cymry.",
  "Yna, yng nghanol y 1160au, cymerodd deitl newydd. Yn ei ail lythyr at Louis galwodd ei hun yn Waliarum princeps, Tywysog Cymru. Dyma’r defnydd hynaf o’r teitl hwnnw gan reolwr o Gymro sydd wedi goroesi yn unman. Yn llythyrau’r Archesgob Thomas Becket ef yw tywysog y Cymry, princeps Wallensium. Galwai’r beirdd a’r cronicl Cymraeg ef yn dywysog. Gydag Owain Gwynedd y mae’r teitl Tywysog Cymru yn dechrau.",
  "Mae tri o’i lythyrau at Ffrainc wedi goroesi, dau at Louis ei hun ac un at ei ganghellor. Ni wyddys am yr un rheolwr o Gymro o’i flaen a estynnodd law at frenin Ffrainc. Ynddynt mae Owain yn adrodd wrth Louis am ryfel 1165, yn cynnig ei gyfeillgarwch a’i help yn erbyn Harri, ac yn gofyn am ei gefnogaeth yntau. Yn 1168 aeth negeswyr Owain, dan arweiniad Arthur o Enlli, esgob Bangor, â’i negeseuon i Ffrainc.",
  "## Pam y gwnaeth hynny",
  "Gall ymddangos yn rhyfedd heddiw, brenin yn cymryd teitl sy’n swnio’n llai. Ym myd Owain y gwrthwyneb oedd yn wir. Mae haneswyr yn rhoi sawl rheswm, ac maent yn cyd-fynd â’i gilydd.",
  "Y cyntaf yw bod brenin wedi mynd yn air rhy fach yng Nghymru. Bu llawer o frenhinoedd yng Nghymru: brenhinoedd Gwynedd, Powys, Deheubarth a Morgannwg, pob un yn rheoli ei wlad ei hun, a bod yn frenin oedd bod yn un o lawer. Golygai princeps rywbeth na allai’r un ohonynt ei hawlio ar ei ben ei hun. Golygai’r cyntaf, yr un a safai uwchlaw’r lleill i gyd, fel y safodd Awgwstws dros Rufain. Dweud yr oedd Owain mai ef a arweiniai’r Cymry i gyd, nid Gwynedd yn unig. Wedi 1165, pan ymgasglodd rheolwyr Cymru gydag ef yng Nghorwen, roedd y ffeithiau’n cadarnhau’r hawl.",
  "> Un rheolwr yn unig a ddaliai’r teitl, yn llythrennol prif reolwr Cymru. | Huw Pryce, hanesydd tywysogion Cymru",
  "Yr ail yw mai cam i fyny ydoedd, nid cam i lawr. Yn Lladin Ewrop y ddeuddegfed ganrif, rheolwr yn ei hawl ei hun oedd princeps, heb arglwydd uwch ei ben yn ei wlad ei hun. Yn Ffrainc Louis VII, y rheolwyr mawr a lywodraethai eu gwledydd eu hunain, dugiaid Normandi ac Aquitaine ac ieirll Fflandrys a Champagne, oedd tywysogion y deyrnas. Drwy gymryd y teitl gosododd Owain ei hun ymhlith rheolwyr mawr Ewrop, a dweud wrth frenin tramor fod gan Gymru ei phen ei hun nad atebai i neb.",
  "Y trydydd yw y byddai brenin Cymru wedi golygu rhyfel agored dros deitlau. Hawliai Harri’r Ail mai ef oedd arglwydd pob rheolwr yng Nghymru, ac yn 1163 roedd wedi gwneud iddynt dalu gwrogaeth iddo yn Woodstock. Byddai brenin coronog ar Gymru gyfan wedi bod yn goron arall yn cystadlu ym Mhrydain, her y byddai Harri, yr Eglwys a llys Ffrainc i gyd wedi’i gwrthwynebu. Gwnâi tywysog yr un hawl i arwain y Cymry, ar ffurf y gallai rheolwyr Ewrop ei derbyn. Er hynny, roedd yn fwy nag y gallai Harri ei oddef.",
  "Y pedwerydd yw ei fod yn cyd-fynd â syniad y Cymry am dywysog. Daw’r gair o tywys: tywysog yw’r un sy’n mynd o flaen y bobl ac yn eu harwain. Galwodd Owain ei hun yn dywysog y Cymry, Wallensium, arweinydd pobl, ac nid arglwydd darn o dir yn unig. Mae cronicl yr oes wedi’i enwi ar ôl arweinwyr o’r fath: Brut y Tywysogion.",
  "## Teitl o Rufain",
  "O Rufain y daeth y gair ei hun. Pan wnaeth Awgwstws ei hun yn feistr ar y byd Rhufeinig wedi 31 CC, ni fynnai ei alw ei hun yn frenin. Roedd y Rhufeiniaid wedi gyrru allan eu brenin olaf, Tarquin Falch, bron i bum can mlynedd ynghynt, a chasâi’r Rhufeiniaid yr union air rex. Felly cymerodd Awgwstws y teitl princeps, y dinesydd cyntaf, gŵr cyntaf y wladwriaeth. Daw o primus, cyntaf, a capere, cymryd: yr un sy’n cymryd y lle cyntaf. Principate yw’r enw ar deyrnasiad Awgwstws a’r ymerawdwyr ar ei ôl, a daeth princeps yn air am y rheolwr goruchaf.",
  "Ysgrifennai clercod Owain yn Lladin, iaith Rhufain a’r Eglwys, a gwyddent beth a gariai’r gair. Nid anghofiodd Cymru Rufain erioed: olrheiniai brenhinoedd Cymru eu llinach i Facsen Wledig, yr ymerawdwr Magnus Maximus, a chofiai’r Cymry mai hwy oedd y Brythoniaid, dinasyddion Rhufain cyn dyfod y Saeson. Roedd rheolwr o Gymro a’i galwai ei hun yn princeps yn hawlio’r teitl awdurdod hynaf yn y byd Lladin: y cyntaf oll, heb neb uwch ei ben.",
  "## Pam roedd y Brenin Harri’n ddig",
  "Nid oedd Harri’r Ail yn hoffi hyn o gwbl. Daliai fod pob rheolwr yng Nghymru yn ddeiliad iddo. Gallai brenin Gwynedd fod yn un deiliad ymhlith llawer. Roedd tywysog y Cymry yn rhywbeth arall: pen cenedl, yn siarad dros Gymru gyfan. Ac roedd Owain yn ysgrifennu at Louis VII, gelyn mawr Harri, y brenin yr oedd Harri ei hun yn talu gwrogaeth iddo am Normandi. Gallai Tywysog Cymru mewn cynghrair â Ffrainc fygwth Harri o’r gorllewin tra pwysai Louis arno yn Ffrainc.",
  "Cyrhaeddodd y ffrae y Pab. Ysgrifennodd Thomas Becket, Archesgob Caergaint, at y Pab Alecsander III fod ‘y Tywysog Owain, fel y’i geilw ei hun’ wedi gwneud y brenin ‘yn ddig ac yn flin iawn’.",
  "Safodd Owain yn gadarn yn erbyn Eglwys Loegr hefyd. Pan aeth esgobaeth Bangor yn wag, ni fynnai dderbyn esgob a ddewiswyd yn Lloegr. Cysegrwyd ei ddewis ei hun, Arthur o Enlli, yn Iwerddon, ac arhosodd eglwys Gwynedd mewn dwylo Cymreig. Ni allai na brenin nac archesgob wneud iddo ildio, a phan fu farw ym mis Tachwedd 1170 fe’i claddwyd yn Eglwys Gadeiriol Bangor.",
  "## Y tywysogion a ddaeth ar ei ôl",
  "Daeth y teitl a ddewisodd Owain yn deitl Cymru. Rheolodd ei ŵyr Llywelyn ab Iorwerth, Llywelyn Fawr, fel tywysog Gogledd Cymru, ac yn Aberdyfi yn 1216 talodd rheolwyr eraill Cymru wrogaeth iddo. O 1230 galwai ei hun yn Dywysog Aberffraw ac Arglwydd Eryri, gan enwi llys brenhinol Gwynedd ar Ynys Môn a’r mynyddoedd wrth ei chalon. Gwnaeth ei fab Dafydd Tywysog Cymru yn deitl ffurfiol iddo yn 1244.",
  "Yna daeth Llywelyn ap Gruffudd, Llywelyn ein Llyw Olaf. Cymerodd y teitl Tywysog Cymru yn 1258, ac ar 29 Medi 1267, yng Nghytundeb Trefaldwyn, cydnabu’r Brenin Harri III o Loegr ef a’i etifeddion yn Dywysog Cymru, gyda gwrogaeth arglwyddi eraill Cymru. Ŵyr Harri’r Ail oedd Harri III. Ganrif wedi i’r taid gynddeiriogi at y teitl, rhoddodd yr ŵyr ei sêl wrtho.",
  "Yn 1400 cyhoeddwyd Owain Glyndŵr yn Dywysog Cymru yng Nglyndyfrdwy, ac aeth y teitl allan dros Gymru unwaith eto. Gydag Owain arall y dechreuodd: Brenin Gwynedd a ddewisodd gael ei alw’n gyntaf y Cymry.",
  "## Ei deulu a’i feirdd",
  "Canodd Gwalchmai ap Meilyr a Chynddelw Brydydd Mawr ei glod, ac roedd ei fab Hywel ab Owain Gwynedd yn un o feirdd gorau’r oes. Yn ôl traddodiad Cymru hwyliodd mab arall, Madog, tua’r gorllewin o Landrillo a chanfod gwlad dros y cefnfor. Drwy ei ŵyr Llywelyn Fawr, rhedai gwaed Owain Gwynedd ym mhob Tywysog Cymru o dŷ Aberffraw."])
